   "path": "SCENE.md",
   "role": "instructions",
   "content": "# Particle Dissolve\n\nThe frame breaks into drifting grey dust, bright parts throwing theirs furthest, with specks that twinkle and a little colour fringing; the next scene coalesces back out of the dust. Runs its WebGL shader over two built-in panels (a stat and a bar chart, then a render queue; text and colours from variables), not over the host's scenes: 60 frames of the first panel, a 36-frame transition, then the second panel. To use it between your own scenes, copy the shader into your composition and feed it your two frames as textures.\n\nCategory: transitions. Length: 5.2 s. WebGL: yes.\n\nUse when:\n- A scene change should read as the picture breaking down into matter and reassembling, not one image fading through another.\n- The moment can afford well over a second; the dust needs time to form, drift and gather.\n- The outgoing scene has bright content worth watching disintegrate: highlights throw their dust furthest.\n\nAvoid when:\n- You only need texture over a plain crossfade: use grain-dissolve.\n- You want an organic flowing break-up rather than a granular one: use perlin-dissolve.\n\nVariables:\n- label (string): Label, default \"Deploys this week\"\n- value (string): Value, default \"2,481\"\n- bars (string): Bars, default \"32,51,43,68,57,86,64,100\"\n- accent (color): Highlight, default \"#a1eebd\"\n- title (string): Title, default \"Render queue\"\n- items (string): Rows, default \"intro-scene.mp4 | Done; feature-tour.mp4 | Rendering; pricing-outro.mp4 | Queued\"\n- background (color): Background, default \"#101828\"\n- particle_size (number, 1-16): Particle size, default 4\n- scatter (number, 0.01-0.4): Scatter, default 0.08\n- shimmer (number, 0-1.5): Shimmer, default 0.6\n- aberration (number, 0-1.5): Aberration, default 0.5\n- direction (enum): Direction, default \"reveal\"\n- stagger (number, 0-0.9): Stagger, default 0.55\n- resolution (number, 0.25-1): Resolution, default 0.75\n\nSource: https://github.com/Remocn/remocn. Licence: MIT. The scene file keeps the upstream notice.\n"
  },
